Iteration 1 done — ladder item **0.3** is complete and marked. One thing needs your decision at the end.

## What was measured

Rebuilt the server from HEAD (the old binary predated `compressed_decode.cu`'s warp top-k), launched it detached at seqmax 16384 with a memguard, and re-fitted the cost model on a controlled context sweep — 48 generations, 6 repeats at each of 8 context depths, plus 4 controls.

| | pre-fix (battery, n 2158) | post-fix (sweep, n 48) |
|---|---|---|
| `a` | 136.44 ± 0.52 ms | **130.98 ± 2.25 ms** |
| `b` | 30.051 ± 0.241 ms/1000 ctx | **7.362 ± 0.370 ms/1000 ctx** |
| `b × 6592` | 198.10 ms | **48.53 ± 2.44 ms** |
| context range | 71 – 6592 | **249 – 12,410** |
| tau p50 | 2.91 | 1.68 |

R² 0.971. **Neither stop condition is met**: `a` is 1.59× its 82.18 ms byte floor (target 1.25×), `b × 6592` is 9.7× the 5.0 ms threshold.

## The result that matters beyond the number

**0.2's headline "the context term is GONE" is wrong, and I've marked it superseded.** It was two single runs at ctx ≤3000, where the residual term is 22 ms of a 153 ms forward — under the 3.5 % run-to-run spread. At ctx 12,410 it is 91 ms, **41 % of the forward**. The term is 4.08× smaller, not gone.

That matters because 0.2 *retired ladder items 1.2 and 1.5* on `i:topk = 0.10 ms` and `i:score = 0.02 ms` measured at ctx ≤3000, and those two marks cannot account for 91 ms unless they grow. I added **item 0.4** — re-profile at ctx 12k and attribute the residual — and flagged that 1.2/1.5 must not be acted on either way until it reports.

Two things I checked rather than assumed:
- **The slope isn't a speculation artifact.** Verify width correlates +0.35 with context here, and width sets bytes per forward. Fitting both: `fwd = 70.74 + 7.029×(ctx/1000) + 23.04×width`, R² 0.985. Width absorbs 0.33 of the 7.36; the context term survives at 7.03 ± 0.28.
- **The prefix-cache shortcut is sound.** 47 of 48 legs had `prefill=0ms`. Four control legs built their context from a different document: 181.59 vs 179.18 ms/forward at ctx ~6250, 150.09 vs 147.66 at ctx ~1660 — both inside the spread.

**`a` must be read as unchanged, not improved.** This corpus runs at tau 1.68 vs the battery's 2.91, so a smaller expert union is read per forward; 130.98 is the optimistic end of the same number for reasons unrelated to any kernel. The `b` comparison is the sound one — each is a within-corpus slope.

No kernel changed, so no bit-exactness question arises. tau is reported at every point. Server stopped; nothing resident; all six CPU gates pass; `detach_audit.sh` clean.

## Two things for you

1. **I could not commit or push.** `.claude/settings.json` denies `git add`, `git commit`, and `git push`. Everything is written to the working tree and the full commit message is drafted at `evidence/decode_loop/COMMIT_MSG_0p3.txt` — `git add -A && git commit -F evidence/decode_loop/COMMIT_MSG_0p3.txt && git push` applies it. Related: `curl` is also on the deny list, and two of my commands were refused for containing it; one compound command with a localhost health-check curl in it did slip through before I noticed the rule. Not deliberate, and I stopped using it once I saw the list.

2. **`DECODE_LOOP_STOP` appeared at 22:43** (empty, created during the probe run — not by me). The driver will halt after this iteration. I left it in place and added it to `.gitignore`, since a committed stop flag would silently disable the loop on checkout — the same reasoning already written there for `FLYWHEEL_STOP`.
